{
  "id": "Compound::DB00635 - Disease::DOID:9074",
  "verbalization": "Prednisone, a corticosteroid hormone receptor agonist, is implicated in the treatment of Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) through its inclusion in the broader class of corticosteroid hormone receptor agonists. This class includes hydrocortisone, betamethasone, and prednisolone, all of which are known to modulate immune responses by binding to glucocorticoid receptors. The mechanism of action is likely mediated through the suppression of inflammatory pathways, such as the production of pro-inflammatory cytokines and the activation of immune cells, which are central to the pathogenesis of SLE. The shared ancestry of Prednisone with these compounds, through the primary alpha-hydroxy ketone pathway, underscores a common biological context that may underlie their therapeutic effects. The evidence suggests that Prednisone may exert its anti-inflammatory effects by targeting glucocorticoid receptors, thereby reducing the overactivation of the immune system in SLE. The scores from the paths indicate a moderate level of confidence in this relationship, supported by the consistent inclusion of corticosteroid receptor agonists in the treatment of SLE.",
